<a class="u-url sr-only" href="{{ page.url }}">{{ title }}</a>{% comment %} Visual fix: sr-only so the permalink's own title isn't printed twice (h1 above already carries p-name) — see types/article.liquid's comment. {% endcomment %}
{%- if date %}<time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ date | isoInstant }}">{{ date | humanDate }}</time>{% endif %}
{%- comment %} Task 18 (§7.4): see types/article.liquid for why h2,h3. {% endcomment -%}
<div class="content-grid e-content"><heading-anchors selector="h2,h3">{{ content }}</heading-anchors></div>
{%- else -%}
{%- comment %} Task (visual fix, corrected from the prior S4 pass): a note is
titleless by IndieWeb design — eleventyComputed.js's fallback title is the
generic "Note · <date>" label, needed only so the Atom feed's atom:title is
never empty. That label duplicates the adjacent <time>, so it's never shown
— but the prior fix hid the ENTIRE anchor (.sr-only), which also matches
none of the source's `.posts .posts-link` styling: the row rendered as a
bare dotted rule with nothing on either end. The source's `.posts` system is
a link list — `.posts-link` is the visible anchor the dotted leader runs
from — so a note's own substance (its text, truncated to a single line, not
the full 280-char tweet-length default the `truncate` filter otherwise
uses) becomes the visible link text instead of the synthetic label,
matching `.posts-link` the same way article/bookmark's real titles already
do. `.posts .date` (sass/theme-posts.scss) no longer hides <time> in this
port — see that rule's own comment for why. {% endcomment -%}
<a class="posts-link u-url" href="{{ page.url }}">{{ content | strip_html | truncate: 70, false }}</a>
{{ socialHtml }}
{%- if date %}<time class="date dt-published" datetime="{{ date | isoInstant }}">{{ date | listingDate }}</time>{% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
